;; The same mistake with a *concrete* receiver, which the inliner can resolve.
;;
;; This is the fixture that pins the rule, and it was a real bug: the inliner
;; spliced the implementation's body at the call site, which dissolved the call,
;; and with no call left there was nothing for the colour check to look at. The
;; program was accepted. The identical source one file over — same trait, same
;; method, generic receiver — was refused.
;;
;; A program whose acceptance depends on whether the inliner could see the
;; implementor is the thing this compiler avoids everywhere else, so a
;; suspending method is never spliced: it lands on the implementation's own
;; method, named directly, and the call survives to be checked. The saving given
;; up is negligible against what is left, an await being a state-machine
;; transition either way.
;;
;; The message must name 'fetch' and not the pad it landed on. A stack trace may
;; say `Fetcher_System_Int32.Instance::fetch`; a compiler error may not, because
;; that name appears in no source file.

;; EXPECT-ERROR: calling 'fetch' is a yield point
;; EXPECT-ERROR: is defined with (defun ...)

(import (std prelude))

(def/trait (Fetcher %a)
  (: fetch (-bjo-> %a string)))

(impl (Fetcher int)
  (defbjo (fetch x) (int->string x)))

(: bad (-> int string))
(defun (bad n) (fetch n))

(defun (main args) 0)
